[Music]  during the second world war nazi germany  made enemies of europe and much of the  world  but the regime had another lesser-known  foe the rising threat from a deadly  disease  dana jacobson traveled to philadelphia  to speak with author sam  apple who uncovered a fascinating  connection between the nazis  a jewish scientist and a breakthrough  cancer research  that's still being studied today cancer  had really become a german obsession by  the 1920s  it was almost a panic and i think that's  in large part because the disease had  become so much more common  by the 1930s cancer was the second  leading cause of death in germany  and a grave concern for the country's  ruling party  the nazis nobody understood why it was  happening i mean to this day we don't  fully understand and that's a lot of  what my book is about that book  by author sam apple is titled ravenous  otto warburg the nazis and the search  for the cancer diet connection  take me back then and introduce me to  otto  warburg who was he sure otto warburg  was a brilliant german scientist he was  born in the late 19th century  and his father was a famous jewish  physicist emil warburg  einstein and max planck and fritz haber  and emil  fisher all these world famous scientists  are in warburg's house when he's growing  up  and warburg idolizes these men and and  decides that  you know he's going to be a great  scientist too and that's what  warburg did in 1931 he was awarded the  nobel prize for his research on cellular  respiration  his discovery that cancer cells take up  nutrients and eat in a different way  from  other cells became known as the warburg  effect  technically what the warburg effect is  that it was understood  that a human cell could turn to  fermentation  but it was thought that's something that  a cell only does in desperation that's  something that a cell does when it  doesn't have oxygen and actually  if you exercise really intensely and you  start breathing and your lungs are heavy  you know you don't have enough oxygen to  sort of run respiration so your cells do  turn  to fermentation that's but the warburg  effect is really he discovered that  cancer cells were doing this even though  you know there was oxygen available  warburg continued his research as the  nazis strengthened their grip on germany  he refused to leave his homeland even  though he was jewish  and gay living openly with his male  partner  he said i was here before hitler who's  going to tell me to get out of my  institute he had this gorgeous institute  that had built for him two years earlier  but it's built with money from the  rockefeller foundation and he said  they can't touch me as others attempted  to appease the nazis  warburg provoked them so these nazi  brown shirts would come to his institute  and harass him and at one point he said  you know i'm going to burn down my  institute if you come here again how  dare you show up here  you refused to give the nazi salute you  refused to put up the nazi flags that  all the other institutes were doing  how did he survive and not get sent to a  concentration camp as other jews or  homosexuals would have been yeah  and so it really comes to a head in 1941  he knows he stayed too long he knows he  should have left and  they call him into hitler's chance  theory this is  like the truly the belly of the beast  you know they tell him we're going to  let you live  but you're going to focus on cancer  apple says the timing of the meeting  shows just how  serious the nazis were in their  offensive on the disease  it was hours before operation barbarossa  started  this was the biggest moment in the nazi  project they were going to invade the  soviet union  hours and this is what they're talking  about hours before cancer research in  warburg  in his own research apple uncovered  another surprise  hitler's obsession with cancer was  personal  historians say that his mother was the  only person he loved the only person he  had a relationship with  and she died of breast cancer how did  did that manifest itself later on with  the nazi party  cancer was one more impurity and the  jews were one more impurity for hitler  and the nazis so  in a way it fits into this dark sort of  outlook that they had was that hard when  you're writing about that to wrap your  head around  some of it yeah it it was very hard  like most jews i you know lost family  members and the holocaust and  anytime you're researching these things  it's difficult  but it also deeply uncomfortable to i'm  so  repulsed by the nazis to the core of my  being to say that they made  any sort of progress in cancer yeah i  hate saying that  six million jews were persecuted and  murdered by the nazi regime  and its allies and collaborators warburg  would survive the war leaving many in  the scientific community  to wonder how before the war you know it  had been a major  ideas about cancer that it involves this  metabolic shift  and then after the war i'd say in the  1950s it's still around but it starts to  to sort of fade away and there's a bunch  of different reasons for that but one of  the  kind of shocking ones is that people  suspected warburg for having stayed in  nazi germany  how did this guy survive did he  collaborate one of the samples by the  mid-70s warburg's metabolic approach was  all but forgotten as  scientists discovered cancer-causing  genes or oncogenes so everyone was now  focusing on oncogenes and what do they  do  and it's taken us a long time to figure  out what they do now we know what they  do  is they pretty much alter metabolism  and so which brings you back to brings  back to otter warburg right  dr louis cantley is the director of the  meyer cancer center at weill cornell  he discovered a previously unknown  enzyme  pi3k we're heading into the tissue  culture room  so these are the incubators where we  grow the cancer cells  sam apple approached dr cantley while  researching his book to ask a critical  question  could our diets be causing cancer i  think sugar is the most dangerous food  we eat  so when you consume fructose and it gets  to the liver gives you fatty liver  you become insulin resistant and that  means ambient insulin levels are high  all the time you know my lab discovered  that insulin  activates this enzyme pi3k  and the gene for that is one of the most  frequently mutated  genes in cancer and it's activated by  insulin so if your insulin resistant  and you pick up one of these mutations  you have a very good chance that that's  going to drive the growth of that tumor  author sam apple found that dr cantley  is just one of a number of scientists  concerned about  sugar and cancer my goal in writing  my book ravenous was really to see if  there's a way to connect these stories  to  to put warburg into the perspective of  the whole diet story  you know my research and talking to many  cancer scientists really  came down to sugar in a very fundamental  way modern theories  built on more than a century of science  actually been this tremendous metabolism  revival you know it started in in the  late 1990s and now they have conferences  and new  metabolism centers are opening and it's  really  fundamentally changed cancer science so  you know warburg was lost and  and now he he's been rediscovered a book  i did not know about beforehand but  we'll  but we'll check out now sounds  fascinating thank you dana remarkable  legacy too to think about  all of the conflict he must have felt  you know  working on this but at the same time  knowing he was working on the project he  wanted to advance the science  and advancing it yeah we're still  talking about it today so glad we  rediscovered this  this lost history for sure

